      "sequence_position": 12,
      "problem": "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ What is $(\\frac{1}{2}a)\\cdot b?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, multiplying each component of $a$ by $\\frac{1}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}a & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{2}a)\\cdot b & =2\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+3+1+0+10 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$",
